#NAME
        _printf()
#SYNOPSIS
        #include "main.h"
        int _printf(const char *format, ...)
#DESCRIPTION
        The _printf() function writes formatted output to the standard
        output. It is a simplified reimplementation of the standard printf
        function.

        The format string contains ordinary characters, which are written
       directly to the output, and conversion specifications, which start
       with a '%' character.

        A conversion specification has the following form:
        %specifier
        Common conversion specifiers include:
	- d, i : signed decimal integer
	- c : character
	- s : string
#RETURN VALUE
          On success, _printf() returns the total number of characters
       written.

       On error, a negative value may be returned (depending on the
       implementation).
#EXAMPLES
        _printf("Hello %s\n", "world");
              Writes: Hello world

        _printf("Value: %d\n", 42);
              Writes: Value: 42    
